[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router1
diagnosis_text:
Router0 is routing correctly; the outage is caused by a firewall rule on router1 dropping all forwarded traffic. Evidence:
- Router0 has correct RIPv2 routes to 1.0.0.0/8 and 128.168.0.0/16 via router1 (show ip route and show ip rip on router0). IT/CS hosts can reach router0 and even router1’s own IPs (e.g., it_lab1 -> 192.168.3.1 OK), proving router0 is routing its local subnets.
- From router0, pings/traceroutes to 1.0.0.1 and 128.168.0.2 hit 10.10.0.2 (router1) as hop 1 and then die. From router1, pings to 1.0.0.5 and 1.0.0.1 succeed, proving router2 and Server/Internet Lab are up and reachable from router1 itself.
- On router1, IP forwarding is enabled (cat /proc/sys/net/ipv4/ip_forward = 1) but iptables shows “-A FORWARD -j DROP”, which drops all transit packets regardless of routing (INPUT/OUTPUT chains are ACCEPT, so only forwarded traffic is impacted). This exactly matches the symptom: local-to-router1 traffic works, any traffic that must traverse router1 is blocked.

Affected component: router1 firewall (iptables FORWARD chain). Mechanism: all forwarded packets are dropped at router1, preventing IT/CS traffic from reaching router2’s 1.0.0.x and 128.168.0.x networks. Localization: router1, FORWARD chain. Root cause: a misconfigured iptables rule “-A FORWARD -j DROP” on router1. Corrective fix: remove/flush the drop rule and permit forwarding (e.g., iptables -D FORWARD -j DROP or iptables -F FORWARD; optionally add explicit ACCEPT rules between eth2<->eth3 and to router2). Ensure the fix is made persistent across reboots.
=== END ===